          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does osteoarthritis guidance describe as core treatment before anything else?",
          "answer": "**Core treatment: exercise, first.** The osteoarthritis guidance (*Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management and the sequence of treatment options*) places non-drug management first. It says structured exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence of any intervention for knee and hip osteoarthritis, with effect sizes comparable to medicines and a much better safety profile. The program combines muscle strengthening around the joint, movement exercises, and aerobic activity. It says supervision at the start helps because correct progression determines the result. The guidance also says exercise helps regardless of X-ray severity, age, or starting pain. The general reference on osteoarthritis (*Osteoarthritis*) adds weight management where relevant, plus education, pacing, suitable footwear, walking aids, and heat or cold for symptom relief.\n\n**Medicines are described as add-ons, not replacements.** The same guidance describes topical anti-inflammatory creams or gels as the preferred first medicine for knee and hand osteoarthritis. It describes oral anti-inflammatory drugs as effective but needing a risk check first. It describes steroid injections into the joint as short-term relief for flares, and duloxetine for widespread pain. It says opioids are not recommended, and that glucosamine and chondroitin are not recommended by most bodies.\n\n**Where authorities differ.** The guidance flags a live disagreement over intra-articular steroid injections. Some bodies recommend them conditionally for short-term relief. Others are more cautious, citing modest duration of benefit and evidence that repeated injections may be linked to cartilage loss. Bodies also differ on topical capsaicin and on whether platelet-rich plasma and similar injections should be listed as options or discouraged.\n\n**On evidence strength.** The guidance gives exercise the strongest evidence, but the retrieved passages do not include a formal evidence grading scale, so I cannot give specific grades for the other options.\n\n**Not covered.** My searches returned nothing on paracetamol (acetaminophen), so I cannot say what guidance says about it.\n\nA hot, swollen, painful joint with fever needs prompt medical assessment, because it may not be osteoarthritis. Please discuss any treatment choice with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
